          "block_content": "5. A particle moves on the cartesian plane in such a way that from point  $ (a, b) $ it can go to either  $ (a+1, b) $ or  $ (a, b+1) $. Given  $ n \\in \\mathbb{N} $, let  $ a_n $ be the number of distinct ways the particle has to go from  $ A_0(0, 0) $ to  $ A_n(n, n) $, without ever touching a point  $ (x, y) $ situated above the bisector of odd quadrants (i.e., one such point for which  $ y > x $). In this respect, do the following items:",
